WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.035
I want to start with the spectacle

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.870
we saw on the House floor

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.538
a couple of weeks ago.

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:08.174
Republicans taking 15 votes

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:11.511
to elect Kevin McCarthy speaker.

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:13.179
Forget the partizanship,

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.948
republican versus Democrat.

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:17.584
As a political pro watching that.

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:19.119
What did you think?

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:21.254
Well, I was sad for the institution.

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:22.922
They should have had their act together.

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:24.557
They should have gotten it done.

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:26.559
And that was sad.

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:30.463
It was nothing to be amused by

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:32.365
or laugh at or any of

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:34.534
it was sad for the institution.

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:36.302
So was it worth his doing it?

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.705
I mean, yes, it was kind of ugly.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:41.574
And and not particularly dignified.

00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:42.776
But he did get elected

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:43.476
speaker in the House.

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:44.611
Well, 15 times.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:46.246
I mean, that's kind of start.

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:48.348
I would hope that he would get it done

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:49.382
right. From the start.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.750
What's the challenge?

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.919
Let's figure this out. Let's get it done.

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:54.654
And if not, let's move on

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:56.022
to someone else.

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:57.190
I want to pick up on that.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.224
Your daughter,

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.392
Alexander, has done

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.294
a fascinating documentary

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:01.728
about

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.329
you called Pelosi in the House,

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.465
which is running right now on HBO, Max.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:08.668
And in it, she shows you rounding up

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:11.738
votes back in 2018 to be elected speaker.

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:12.372
Take a look.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.075
I have a good feel

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:17.177
for where the votes are in my caucus.

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:23.983
Since the election,

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.854
she has met in person with 67 members

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:29.055
or members elect.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:30.156
So how do you get people's votes?

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:33.526
Do you just break their knees and make no

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:35.962
And I'm
very respectful of people's views.

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:39.065
So I want to hear what they are.

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:42.202
I want to hear what people have to say.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:46.539
You can vote by listening

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.608
So what would you have done

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:50.009
if you were McCarthy

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.045
and you got to the first day

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:53.847
of the actual session?

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:55.014
You know,
you've gone through

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:56.883
all of this stuff before.

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:58.251
And you didn't have the votes.

00:01:58.251 --> 00:01:59.519
I would have had the votes.

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:00.954
I knew I had the votes.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:01.788
I mean, I don't.

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:02.922
I had

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:06.092
well over 200 votes in the caucus,

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.327
so I knew I would have the votes.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:08.561
It was never a question.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:09.362
What happens?

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.431
Chris, just so you know, is

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:12.332
the press

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.334
makes a big thing of opposition.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:15.301
You know, say,

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.436
oh, so-and-so said

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:17.871
this and so-and-so said that.

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:19.172
But it isn't as

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.709
it's not as it may

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:23.943
sell papers,

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:26.346
but it's not really the true picture.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.448
Of what is happening there.

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:30.717
McCarthy says that he is going to kick

00:02:30.884 --> 00:02:32.418
three Democrats

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.654
off their committees here.

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:35.421
Here they are.

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:39.092
Adam Schiff, Eric Swalwell, Ilhan Omar.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:40.827
And he says

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:42.262
that you set the precedent

00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:44.430
because in the last session,

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.033
the Democrats kicked two Republicans,

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:49.202
Marjorie Taylor Greene

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.304
and Paul Gosar off their committees.

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:53.673
Didn't you open the door to this? No.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.074
We set a precedent,

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:56.543
which I hope that will follow.

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:59.145
If they have members, as they did

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:00.780
with threatened,

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.582
the security of our members

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:03.683
on the committee

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.852
were a danger to our members

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:07.820
threatening them,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.155
then they would go off the committee.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:11.191
So if they have that accusation

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:12.825
about any of our members,

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.527
let's hear what they have to say.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.795
It was clear

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:16.729
that their members

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.364
were a threat to our members.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:21.434
So this is about maintaining safety

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.769
for our members.

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.270
The fact that they want to

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.338
take these people off

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.172
the committee

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:28.608
is more philosophic as philosophical.

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.043
During

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.277
the long vote

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:32.111
for speaker,

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:34.347
millions of Americans were riveted

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:36.549
by the action on the floor

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:38.151
and they saw stuff

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.119
they had never seen before.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.588
As you can see up here on the screen

00:03:41.588 --> 00:03:42.722
now with the speaker

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:45.992
talking to Matt Gaetz and then another

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:47.727
bit hold back.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.729
I mean, it was fascinating stuff.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:52.232
The fact is, though, that normally

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:54.100
once the speaker is in charge,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.368
that C-SPAN is sharply

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:56.769
limited in what it can show.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.304
It basically can show the speaker

00:03:58.304 --> 00:03:59.672
up on the podium,

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:00.340
it can show

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.341
whoever is talking,

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:02.342
which member is talking

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:04.077
on the wall of the House.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.512
There's been a push to change the rules

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:07.247
to allow people

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:09.549
to see what really is going on

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:10.817
on the floor.

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:12.151
Do you support that or not?

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:12.952
Well, it depends.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.387
I don't have any objection

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.489
to transparency and the rest.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:17.590
And you didn't allow it

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:18.625
when you were a speaker.

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:19.292
But I don't

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:20.493
I think it's supposed to be

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:22.695
about following the procedure.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:25.031
The debate in the Congress.

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:27.000
And if there's more

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.835
opportunity for that, fine.

00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:30.570
But I don't think it should be

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:36.676
used as a tool against members.

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:37.844
I saw you talking

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:39.312
to so-and-so on the floor,

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:40.346
and they said that that

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:41.748
that shouldn't be the case.

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:44.417
This was remarkable, because when we

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:45.752
when this was all going

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:47.220
on, you have to remember

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.222
there were no rules in that house. Right.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:50.823
Anything went.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.592
And sometimes they said

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:54.494
terrible things on the Republican side,

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:55.995
but we couldn't challenge it

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:58.831
because there were no rules of the House.

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:00.166
And

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:02.869
forgetting whether it's rules

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:06.239
or not, foot for the potential speaker

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:08.374
or anyone to go up to a member

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:10.176
in that manner.

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:12.211
This just does not bring dignity

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:13.446
to the house of represent.

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:14.580
Let me see if I got this right there.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.015
Basically, you're saying

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:17.617
you liked the rules the way they are.

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.452
Follow the official proceedings.

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:21.187
If there's something going on in

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:22.055
one of the aisles,

00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:23.289
you don't want that on TV.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:24.524
Well, it depends on what it is.

00:05:24.524 --> 00:05:25.158
I don't know.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:26.726
I don't I don't want it to curb the

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.762
party action that might

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:30.997
happen on the floor.

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.665
That might be positive.

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:34.801
But by and large, my view is

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.470
the more transparency, the better.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.472
We now have not one

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:41.474
but two special counsels.

00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:41.941
Yeah.

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.843
One investigating President

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:45.178
Trump, former President Trump.

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:47.113
One investigating current President

00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:48.214
Joe Biden

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:49.282
for their handling

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:51.017
of classified documents.

00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:51.384
Here's

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:53.886
what you had to say about Donald Trump

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:55.621
and his problems this summer.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:56.589
Take a look.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.960
If the nature of this

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:05.498
of these documents is what appears to be,

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:08.267
this is very serious.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.973
Do you think that classified documents

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:18.611
showing up in Joe Biden's office home

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:19.545
six years

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:20.747
after he was vice president,

00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:22.682
do you think that's also very serious?

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:23.883
Well,
it depends on the nature

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:24.617
of the documents.

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:25.918
What I said

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:26.419
as you were

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:26.953
listening

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:29.589
was if the nature of these documents

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:32.058
is what it appears to be, we don't know.

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:33.926
But what we were talking about,

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:35.094
it was the highest level

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.963
of classification of the documents.

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:39.432
And so I think you have to talk about

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.835
the procedure.

00:06:42.835 --> 00:06:45.104
President Biden has said

00:06:45.104 --> 00:06:47.206
his lawyers are finding these

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:48.307
and bringing them out

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:50.576
President

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.945
Trump was obstructing

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:54.680
obstructing access to them.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.182
So I think you look at volume,

00:06:56.182 --> 00:06:57.750
you look at procedure,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.252
and then you have to see

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:00.753
what the nature of the driver.

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:02.455
But we don't know what the nature is.

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:03.322
But you said it

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:05.057
perfectly in the beginning.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:07.794
There are two special counsels.

00:07:07.794 --> 00:07:09.028
Let the let the truth

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:11.130
come forth by the special counsel's.

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:12.432
But you're

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:13.366
talking about the nature

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:13.966
of the documents,

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:15.234
if the documents turned out

00:07:15.234 --> 00:07:17.136
to be very sensitive.

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:19.572
The Biden documents

00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:20.706
that would be very unusual.

00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:22.942
Don't I said they're if.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:25.478
Right.
Yeah, that would be very serious.

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:26.813
Well, we'll see what they are.

00:07:26.813 --> 00:07:30.016
I don't think that having a

00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:32.785
briefing on a meeting with somebody,

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:34.253
you know.

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:35.421
We used to tease up

00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:37.590
an intelligence committee and just say,

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:38.191
be careful

00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:39.725
because they're going to stamp

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:41.761
classified on The Washington Post.

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:44.030
I understand that the cases are

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:46.098
very different. You talk about

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:49.368
the number of documents where they were,

00:07:49.368 --> 00:07:50.903
the question of transparency

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:53.105
and cooperation by the the two men.

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:54.874
You said it very well.

00:07:54.874 --> 00:07:55.741
Thank you.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.711
But as a practical matter,

00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:01.347
isn't it going to be impossible,

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:03.583
even if the facts were to bear out

00:08:03.583 --> 00:08:05.518
that Trump

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:07.620
committed a crime and should be charged?

00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:08.554
Isn't it

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:10.223
as a practical matter, impossible,

00:08:10.223 --> 00:08:12.258
given the fact that Biden

00:08:12.258 --> 00:08:13.226
had documents

00:08:13.226 --> 00:08:15.995
and his office had documents in his home?

00:08:15.995 --> 00:08:17.396
Well, it depends.

00:08:17.396 --> 00:08:17.830
It depends.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.233
And that's what you have a special.

00:08:20.233 --> 00:08:21.667
I think that again,

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:22.435
I said you said it

00:08:22.435 --> 00:08:24.103
perfectly to begin with.

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:25.071
The attorney general

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:27.240
asked someone to review

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:27.807
to see

00:08:27.807 --> 00:08:31.577
if a special prosecutor was recommended.

00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:32.879
He said, yes,

00:08:32.879 --> 00:08:35.114
there was a special prosecutor

00:08:35.114 --> 00:08:36.015
for Joe Biden,

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:37.083
even though the cases

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:38.351
are quite different.

00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:39.151
We don't know

00:08:39.151 --> 00:08:41.120
until the special prosecutor

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.422
does all of the investigation.

00:08:43.422 --> 00:08:45.424
Is it when you say is it harder

00:08:47.793 --> 00:08:49.328
I don't know.

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:49.762
I don't know.

00:08:49.762 --> 00:08:51.163
It just depends on what comes

00:08:51.163 --> 00:08:52.465
out of the investigations.

